<p>Today's brief covers stories from 16 to 17 November.</p>

<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Sign up</b><span>An attacker abuses a flaw in a vendor's account sign-up flow</span></li>
<li><b>Write</b><span>They gain write access to other customers' projects</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Inject</b><span>Malicious JavaScript is added to a custom code field</span></li>
<li><b>Serve</b><span>The vendor's CDN delivers it to every page of customers' sites</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Steal</b><span>Visitors' payment details and crypto transfers are targeted</span></li>
<li><b>Contain</b><span>The vendor fixes the flaw, removes code and revokes tokens</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>How a flaw in one vendor became malware on 178 organisations' websites, as reported.</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="block">
<h2>1. Attackers inject malware into 178 websites through Optimizely</h2>
<p>Optimizely said an unauthorised party exploited a flaw in its Experimentation account sign-up flow to inject malicious JavaScript into some customer projects; Report URI identified infected snippets across 178 organisations, including major media and technology brands<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The Register reports the code targeted visitors' payment details and cryptocurrency transfers, and Optimizely says it fixed the flaw, removed the code, revoked tokens and notified customers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> any third-party script on your site runs with your site's full trust. List every script you load, keep testing, analytics and marketing tags off payment and login pages, restrict what scripts can do with a Content Security Policy, monitor scripts for unexpected changes and make sure you can switch a vendor's tag off in minutes. Payment card rules already require monitoring of scripts on payment pages; this shows why. See the <a href="/case-studies/indonesia-ewallet-payment-page-script-security/">payment page script security case study</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>2. Nvidia beats and raises in its first results without Huang</h2>
<p>Nvidia reported third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $115.8 billion, with data centre revenue of $107.6 billion, and guided next-quarter revenue to about $130 billion<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. CNBC notes it was the company's first report since Jensen Huang resigned as chief executive, with Colette Kress serving as interim CEO<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> demand for AI hardware is still rising, which means capacity stays tight and expensive for most buyers into next year. Plan for that: commit to capacity only where usage is proven, make models cheaper to run, and keep your stack portable across providers. See <a href="/insights/microsoft-oracle-gpu-capacity-contracts/">AI capacity contracts</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>3. New York creates a unit to enforce its AI safety law</h2>
<p>New York Attorney General Letitia James announced an AI Safety, Standards, Enforcement and Transparency unit, which her office describes as the first in the country dedicated to enforcing AI laws, and which will enforce the state's RAISE Act for frontier AI developers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> enforcement capacity is following the laws. The direct duties fall on large model developers, but expect providers to ask customers for help with incident reporting and to update terms. Keep a record of the AI models you use and how; see the <a href="/insights/board-ai-briefing-checklist/">board AI briefing checklist</a> and the <a href="/fractional-cto-united-states/">US page</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>4. Microsoft releases MAI Code 2 for coding</h2>
<p>Microsoft announced MAI Code 2 and MAI Code 2 Flash at Ignite, available in GitHub Copilot, alongside other new MAI models; Microsoft's own benchmarks claim performance close to leading frontier models at a much lower price<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> coding model choice is becoming a cost lever, not just a quality one. Test new models on your own repositories and tasks before switching, and keep your coding agent policy independent of any one model; see <a href="/insights/ai-coding-policy-engineering-teams/">AI coding policy for engineering teams</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do this week</h2>
<ul>
<li>Inventory third-party scripts on your sites and remove any from payment and login pages.</li>
<li>Add or tighten a Content Security Policy and script change monitoring.</li>
<li>Revisit AI capacity plans in light of continued tight supply.</li>
<li>Trial new coding models on real tasks before changing defaults.</li>
</ul>
<p>I am based in India, the United States and the UAE, working globally. See <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
